\section{Computational efficiency}\label{computational-performance}

The experiments use an RTX 5090, PyTorch 2.12.0+cu130, CUDA 13.0 and CuPy 14.2.0. Each calculation solves the six unit macroscopic-strain load cases with the FP64 true-residual tolerance $\tau=10^{-8}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:true-residual}. We first compare the baseline solvers and preconditioners in Section~\ref{stabilized-voxel-fem-formulation}, then evaluate the three optimizations in Section~\ref{matrix-free-batched-voxel-fem-method}.

Times are medians of three independent process runs, each following a complete warm-up on the same grid. Time whiskers span the three measurements. Peak GPU memory is the largest NVML process-memory sample across these runs, sampled every 20 ms and including the context, libraries and retained allocations. Iteration counts sum the six load cases. Complete-call time covers material preparation, assembly or operator initialization, preconditioner setup, load construction, PCG, FP64 residual checks, stress recovery and cleanup. Geometry generation, compilation, warm-up and file I/O are excluded. Resident operator calls are timed separately with CUDA events.

\subsection{Baseline solvers and FFT preconditioning}\label{sec:isotropic-performance}

The two $192^3$ cells with isotropic constituents in Section~\ref{sec:chfem-verification} compare chfem, conventional assembled finite elements and the 27-point implementation. The assembled baseline stores the global FP64 stiffness matrix in CuPy compressed sparse row (CSR) format and uses the CuPy CG routine with scalar Jacobi preconditioning. Eight-point element contributions are assembled in bounded row batches, with exact zeros removed. CuPy CSR and the 27-point Jacobi implementation apply the same zero-mean projection. The independent verification of this baseline is reported in Section~\ref{sec:chfem-verification}.

Table~\ref{tab:solver-summary} collects the complete results. For the spherical inclusion, the six-load times of chfem, CuPy CSR and the 27-point implementation with Jacobi preconditioning are @@sphere_chfem_time@@, @@sphere_csr_time@@ and @@sphere_jacobi_time@@ s. For Grosmont they are @@grosmont_chfem_time@@, @@grosmont_csr_time@@ and @@grosmont_jacobi_time@@ s. These three implementations store and access the same finite-element couplings differently: chfem reuses phase stiffness data, CSR stores values and column indices, and the stencil stores the nodal blocks with implicit grid connectivity. The complete-time comparison also includes each library's vector updates and reductions. The chfem measurements use its native-core interval, starting from host-resident phase labels and excluding input-file parsing.

Replacing Jacobi with FFT preconditioning in the 27-point implementation reduces the iteration totals from @@sphere_jacobi_iter@@ to @@sphere_fft_iter@@ for the sphere and from @@grosmont_jacobi_iter@@ to @@grosmont_fft_iter@@ for Grosmont. The corresponding complete-call times become @@sphere_fft_time@@ and @@grosmont_fft_time@@ s. This establishes the FFT-preconditioned 27-point baseline for the subsequent operator and storage comparisons.

\subsection{Contributions of the solver components}\label{sec:fi-ablation}

The $192^3$ woven cell in Fig.~\ref{fig:fi-woven-geometry} tests the three optimizations with spatially varying anisotropy. Constituent properties are given in Table~\ref{tab:woven_constituent_properties}, and the geometry is defined in \ref{app:resolution-geometry}. Yarn orientations follow their curved paths, and yarns occupy approximately 19.8\% of the elements. The figure hides the matrix and compares the same local region at $64^3$ and $192^3$.
