The controlled sequence uses the same phase-based reference tensor from \ref{app:solver-reference}, normalized to unit shear coefficient. Physical operators and PCG vectors remain in FP64; the FFT also uses FP64 until the final mixed-precision step. @@fft_iteration_statement@@ Figure~\ref{fig:fi-workflow-cost} summarizes the complete-call time and peak memory along this sequence.

\paragraph{Element constitutive work}
The first comparison follows Section~\ref{sec:fi-modal}. Direct evaluation at the eight Gauss points takes @@direct_Ku@@ ms per stiffness action, whereas the modal implementation takes @@modal_Ku@@ ms with the same element data and nodal accumulation. Relative to the stored 27-point operator, modal evaluation changes complete-call time from @@woven_fft_time@@ to @@modal_time@@ s and peak memory from @@woven_fft_memory@@ to @@modal_memory@@ GiB. The sparse strain-coefficient evaluation reduces local work, while eliminating the spatially varying stencil cache provides the storage saving.

\paragraph{Material sharing and background reuse}
Following Section~\ref{sec:compact-material}, hybrid storage reduces peak memory from @@modal_memory@@ to @@hybrid_memory@@ GiB; complete-call time changes from @@modal_time@@ to @@hybrid_time@@ s. Adding background decomposition then reduces the stiffness-action time from @@hybrid_Ku@@ to @@background_Ku@@ ms and complete-call time to @@background_time@@ s. Material sharing removes repeated tensor data, and the background pass replaces repeated element calculations in the matrix phase with a shared nodal action.

The independent layered-cell comparison in Table~\ref{tab:fi-background-ablation} examines the influence of active volume using the optimized FP32 FFT--PCG configuration. The isotropic phase is the shared background, and the orthotropic phase is supplied through local orientations so that its volume fraction controls the active set. Complete-call time is nearly unchanged at 50.00\% active volume and increases by @@active90_overhead@@\% at 89.84\%. The background pass traverses the full grid, while the contrast pass becomes more expensive as the active set grows.
\input{sections_structured/table_fi_background}

\paragraph{Compact storage and GPU execution}
Section~\ref{sec:solver-workflow} first exploits symmetry in the stored-stencil representation. In its paired comparison, compressing 27 blocks to 14 changes peak memory from @@woven_fft_memory@@ to @@symmetric_memory@@ GiB and stiffness-action time from @@stencil_Ku@@ to @@symmetric_Ku@@ ms. This pair quantifies symmetry reuse within the stencil implementation.

For the background--modal solver, reference-coefficient caching, FFT-plan and workspace reuse, and fused PCG updates reduce complete-call time from @@background_time@@ to @@execution_time@@ s. A preconditioner call changes from @@background_preconditioner@@ to @@execution_preconditioner@@ ms. These changes reduce repeated reference evaluation and intermediate-array transfers. Switching only the FFT buffers, spectra and cached coefficients to FP32 gives the final @@mixed_time@@ s and @@mixed_memory@@ GiB. The FP64 and FP32 execution variants require @@precision_iterations@@ iterations; their maximum paired effective-stiffness difference is @@precision_error@@. Physical stiffness actions, PCG vectors and residual checks remain in FP64.

