\subsection{Overall performance and grid size}\label{sec:execution-results}

The woven geometry in \ref{app:resolution-geometry} was sampled on seven $N^3$ grids, with $N=64,96,\ldots,256$. Grid convergence is assessed using Eq.~\eqref{eq:relative_frobenius_difference} with the $256^3$ stiffness as the reference (Fig.~\ref{fig:fi-grid-scaling}a). The discrepancy decreases overall from 0.8854\% at $64^3$ to 0.0777\% at $224^3$ and remains below 0.09\% over $160^3$--$224^3$. At each resolution, the interfaces are resampled from the same analytical geometry alongside refinement of the displacement field.

The resolution study uses FP32 FFT in all four implementations. The two stored-stencil solvers use the sampled reference medium, whereas the two modal solvers use the phase-based reference in \ref{app:solver-reference}. The proposed solver has the lowest complete-call time and peak GPU memory over their common $64^3$--$224^3$ range (Fig.~\ref{fig:fi-grid-scaling}b,c). Stored stencil blocks and element-wise material tensors grow with grid size, widening the memory gap. At $256^3$, the proposed solver completes the six load cases in 4.387 s using 4.764 GiB.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\makebox[\linewidth][c]{\includegraphics{fi_grid_scaling_en.pdf}}
\caption{Grid convergence and computational cost.}
\label{fig:fi-grid-scaling}
\end{figure}
